## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

---

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props or state change. For primitives, this is value equality (`"hello" === "hello"`). For objects and functions, it's **reference equality** — a new object literal is always a new reference, even if contents are identical.

```jsx
// Re-renders Child every time Parent renders — new object reference each time
function Parent() {
  return <Child style={{ color: "red" }} onClick={() => doSomething()} />;
}
```

Every render of `Parent` creates a brand-new `{}` and `() => {}`, so `Child` always sees "new" props.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

| Tool | Memoizes | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `React.memo` | A **component** — skips re-render if props are shallowly equal | Child is pure and renders often with same props |
| `useMemo` | A **computed value** — recomputes only when deps change | Expensive calculation, or object/array passed as prop |
| `useCallback` | A **function reference** — stable across renders | Function passed as prop to a memoized child |

**These only help when the child is wrapped in `React.memo`.** `useCallback` alone does nothing if the child always re-renders anyway.

```jsx
// useCallback is useless here — Child isn't memoized
const Child = ({ onClick }) => <button onClick={onClick}>Click</button>;
```

Avoid premature memoization — it adds overhead and complexity. Profile first.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer re-renders whenever the context **value reference** changes. The classic mistake:

```jsx
// Bad — new object on every render, all consumers re-render
function AuthProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </AuthContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

Fix with `useMemo`:

```jsx
// Good — stable reference unless user changes
function AuthProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
  return <AuthContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</AuthContext.Provider>;
}
```

For large contexts, split into separate contexts by update frequency (e.g., separate `UserContext` from `ThemeContext`) so unrelated consumers don't re-render.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every keystroke in the search input:

```jsx
function Dashboard() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState("");
  const [items, setItems] = useState([...largeDataset]);

  const filtered = items.filter(i => i.name.includes(query)); // recalculates every render
  const handleSelect = (id) => console.log("selected", id);  // new ref every render

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={filtered} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = ({ items, onSelect }) => { /* heavy render */ };
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `filtered` or `handleSelect` actually change:

```jsx
function Dashboard() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState("");
  const [items, setItems] = useState([...largeDataset]);

  const filtered = useMemo(
    () => items.filter(i => i.name.includes(query)),
    [items, query]
  );

  const handleSelect = useCallback(
    (id) => console.log("selected", id),
    [] // stable — no deps
  );

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={filtered} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = React.memo(({ items, onSelect }) => { /* heavy render */ });
```

**The three-part contract:** `useMemo` stabilizes the value, `useCallback` stabilizes the function, and `React.memo` makes the child actually skip the render. All three must be present for the optimization to work.
